<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wolgast</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wolgast is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, on the Peenestrom opposite the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usedom">Usedom</a>. From 1295 it was the residence of the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-wolgast">Pomerania-Wolgast</a>, whose castle no longer survives. It was Swedish from 1648 to 1815 and is known today for its shipyard.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wolgast.jpg" alt="Wolgast" width="320" height="251" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Niteshift · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wolgast_Rathausplatz_2013-06-21_25c.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Wolgast lies in the district of Vorpommern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greifswald">Greifswald</a>, on the Peenestrom, facing the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/usedom">Usedom</a>. A movable bascule bridge known as the Blaues Wunder gives access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> by road and railway. The town was founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wends">Wends</a> on an island in the sound. On the mainland opposite the island with the castle a new planned town was built in the course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung">Ostsiedlung</a>, the German settlement eastwards.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The occupation of the region by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartislaw-i-duke-of-pomerania">Wartislaw I, Duke of Pomerania</a>, led to the conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-of-bamberg">Otto of Bamberg</a> in 1128, and thousands of Low German settlers came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drenthe">Drenthe</a>. Wolgast became the seat of a Pomeranian castellany and was important in the 12th-century warfare between Pomeranians, Rani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>. A Danish-Rani fleet attacked it in 1162, and the Danes attacked again in 1167 and 1168. After a failed assault in 1177 and a campaign in 1179 in which the fleet accepted money instead of a surrender, it came under Danish control in 1185, when the Pomeranian duke accepted Danish suzerainty. It remained a Danish bridgehead after the Danes lost most of Pomerania in 1227.</p><p>The date of the German town law is not known, but a letter written in or before 1259 confirms the town, and Duke Bogislaw IV confirmed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> law in 1282. Wolgast was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania-wolgast">Pomerania-Wolgast</a>, an inner partition of the duchy, and its castle was built as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> residential palace on what is called Castle Island. The ducal line of Pomerania-Wolgast became extinct when Philipp Julius died without issue.</p><p>Between the 1670s and the 1720s hundreds of men of the town enlisted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> and emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Russian forces burned the town in 1713 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a>, in retaliation for Swedish arson in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altona">Altona</a>; only the church, four chapels and four other buildings were spared. After the Swedish withdrawal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> in 1815 the town passed to the Prussian Province of Pomerania, and the last remnants of the palace were removed in 1849. In the 19th century Wolgast prospered as a port for the grain trade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Most houses of the Old Town date from the 18th and 19th centuries because of the fire of 1713. St Peter's Church, in brick Gothic, was inaugurated around 1415, and St Gertruden Chapel is a 15th-century Gothic building. The painter Philipp Otto Runge was born in the town, and his house is now a museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wolgast” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The most important partition, in 1295, created the duchies of Pomerania-Stettin and Pomerania-Wolgast; <b>Wolgast</b> gained the Principality of Rügen in 1325 and Stettin was confirmed as an immediate imperial fief, free of Brandenburg overlordship, in 1338.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Duchy of Pomerania · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Bogislaw took the coastal lands north of the Peene and ruled from <b>Wolgast</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-pomerania-wolgast">Duchy of Pomerania-Wolgast</a></li></ul></section>
